1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,022
Quick one.

2
00:00:01,042 --> 00:00:01,471
Listen.

3
00:00:05,591 --> 00:00:06,744
What note comes next?

4
00:00:07,564 --> 00:00:08,344
Take a moment.

5
00:00:08,364 --> 00:00:09,487
Commit to an answer.

6
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:12,467
You just did what AI does all day.

7
00:00:12,487 --> 00:00:14,039
It doesn't know the answer.

8
00:00:14,059 --> 00:00:14,912
It predicts it.

9
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:15,902
Sneaky, isn't it?

10
00:00:16,422 --> 00:00:19,890
With AI set to start from Class
3 in India this school year,

11
00:00:19,910 --> 00:00:24,772
parents keep asking us one thing: what does
my child actually need to know about it?

12
00:00:25,192 --> 00:00:27,598
Here are five habits to try at home.

13
00:00:27,618 --> 00:00:28,138
A phone.

14
00:00:28,158 --> 00:00:29,081
A curious kid.

15
00:00:29,101 --> 00:00:29,823
10 minutes.

16
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:36,162
First, the ground rules.

17
00:00:36,482 --> 00:00:39,449
Most AI chat tools are
built for ages 13 and up.

18
00:00:39,469 --> 00:00:42,622
With younger kids, you hold
the phone and you type.

19
00:00:42,642 --> 00:00:43,929
They do the thinking.

20
00:00:44,349 --> 00:00:46,458
And play the billboard game together.

21
00:00:46,478 --> 00:00:50,545
If you wouldn't put it on a billboard
outside school, don't type it in.

22
00:00:50,565 --> 00:00:52,790
No full names, no addresses, no photos.

23
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:54,425
One.

24
00:00:54,445 --> 00:00:55,486
Guess the next note.

25
00:00:55,906 --> 00:00:58,871
This is a real class
Varchas ran with TomoClub,

26
00:00:58,891 --> 00:01:03,064
where kids built their own songs
in Chrome Music Lab's Song Maker.

27
00:01:03,084 --> 00:01:04,715
Listen to what he notices.

28
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:12,047
[Student's song plays]

29
00:01:12,067 --> 00:01:13,047
[Varchas] Nice.

30
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:15,047
I like the gap in between where it plays

31
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:17,847
and gives something to anticipate as well.

32
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:18,347
Well done.

33
00:01:18,867 --> 00:01:20,299
Something to anticipate.

34
00:01:20,319 --> 00:01:21,691
That's the whole trick.

35
00:01:21,711 --> 00:01:26,533
An AI writes one small piece at a time,
always picking the most likely next one.

36
00:01:26,953 --> 00:01:27,829
Try it at home.

37
00:01:27,849 --> 00:01:32,128
Open Song Maker together - it's free,
in the browser - build three bars,

38
00:01:32,148 --> 00:01:34,278
and let your child guess the fourth.

39
00:01:34,298 --> 00:01:34,875
Then swap.

40
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:37,591
AI goes for the safe note every time.

41
00:01:37,611 --> 00:01:39,845
Notice how often your child doesn't.

42
00:01:41,267 --> 00:01:41,509
Two.

43
00:01:41,529 --> 00:01:42,429
Play it twice.

44
00:01:42,849 --> 00:01:46,137
Musicians replay a tricky
bit to check it holds up.

45
00:01:46,157 --> 00:01:50,418
Ask an AI a question, then open a
brand-new chat and ask it again.

46
00:01:50,838 --> 00:01:54,058
If a name, a number or a date
changes, it was guessing.

47
00:01:54,078 --> 00:01:57,710
Give your child a word for that:
a rumour, until you check it.

48
00:01:59,133 --> 00:01:59,463
Three.

49
00:01:59,483 --> 00:02:00,222
Flip the key.

50
00:02:00,642 --> 00:02:05,044
Take a song from a major key to a
minor key, and the whole mood changes.

51
00:02:05,064 --> 00:02:06,764
Questions work the same way.

52
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:09,346
Ask: why is homework good for kids?

53
00:02:09,366 --> 00:02:12,464
Then, in a new chat: why
is homework bad for kids?

54
00:02:12,884 --> 00:02:16,258
If it happily agrees both
times, it's following your lead.

55
00:02:16,278 --> 00:02:20,412
Neutral questions get better answers,
like: what are the pros and cons?

56
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:22,121
Four.

57
00:02:22,141 --> 00:02:23,413
Find the sheet music.

58
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:27,514
Before your child repeats an AI
fact - in a project, in class,

59
00:02:27,534 --> 00:02:29,843
at the dinner table - find the receipt.

60
00:02:30,263 --> 00:02:34,351
A real book or a site you trust,
and the exact line that backs it up.

61
00:02:34,371 --> 00:02:35,006
No receipt?

62
00:02:35,026 --> 00:02:35,959
Don't repeat it.

63
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:37,703
Five.

64
00:02:37,723 --> 00:02:38,673
Play it by ear.

65
00:02:39,093 --> 00:02:43,076
A musician who can only play while reading
the sheet hasn't learned the song yet.

66
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:45,558
So after AI helps with homework,

67
00:02:45,578 --> 00:02:50,114
close the tab and ask your child to
explain it out loud in 30 seconds.

68
00:02:50,534 --> 00:02:53,549
Wherever they get stuck is the
part they still need to learn.

69
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:57,728
Well, the kids in this class
didn't aim for perfect.

70
00:02:57,748 --> 00:02:58,714
They experimented.

71
00:02:59,717 --> 00:03:00,997
[Varchas] Wow, wow. I love this.

72
00:03:01,017 --> 00:03:04,997
Yeah, that's how you experiment.

73
00:03:05,017 --> 00:03:07,497
That's how you experiment. Yes, I love it.

74
00:03:07,517 --> 00:03:08,997
Let's see how it would sound.

75
00:03:09,017 --> 00:03:10,397
[Student's song plays]

76
00:03:10,617 --> 00:03:13,097
I love the pattern in
which you have coloured,

77
00:03:13,117 --> 00:03:15,097
shows that you have gone for some pattern.

78
00:03:15,117 --> 00:03:18,897
This is like pressing all the keys on
the keyboard together, so that's nice.

79
00:03:18,917 --> 00:03:20,997
Yeah, lovely.

80
00:03:21,117 --> 00:03:22,497
Like the experimentation.

81
00:03:22,817 --> 00:03:23,697
Wonderful team.

82
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:25,938
That's the habit worth building.

83
00:03:25,958 --> 00:03:30,044
Treat AI like an instrument your child
is learning, and keep testing it.

84
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:32,468
Now, your turn.

85
00:03:32,488 --> 00:03:34,372
Can your child make an AI flip?

86
00:03:34,792 --> 00:03:38,762
Pick a this-or-that together, like
cats or dogs, or pizza or dosa.

87
00:03:39,182 --> 00:03:41,468
Ask an AI why the first one is better.

88
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:45,414
Then open a brand-new chat, and
ask why the second one is better.

89
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:48,785
Did it flip to agree both
times, or hold its ground?

90
00:03:48,805 --> 00:03:53,586
Tell us in the comments: flipped or held,
and what your child said when they saw it.

91
00:03:54,006 --> 00:03:56,204
Just keep your child's
name and school out of it.

92
00:03:57,933 --> 00:03:58,737
Quick recap.

93
00:03:59,007 --> 00:04:00,014
Guess the next note.

94
00:04:00,234 --> 00:04:01,080
Play it twice.

95
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:02,094
Flip the key.

96
00:04:02,314 --> 00:04:03,407
Find the sheet music.

97
00:04:03,627 --> 00:04:04,666
And play it by ear.

98
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,788
If you'd like your child
to learn this properly,

99
00:04:08,808 --> 00:04:11,453
that's what AI Labs at All
Things Classroom is for.

100
00:04:11,873 --> 00:04:15,990
Live online classes with a real
teacher, the same one every week,

101
00:04:16,010 --> 00:04:17,708
built around making things.

102
00:04:17,728 --> 00:04:19,045
Monthly, no lock-ins.

103
00:04:19,465 --> 00:04:23,693
Tap the link in the description, tell
us about your child, tick AI Labs,

104
00:04:23,713 --> 00:04:25,345
and our team will email you.

105
00:04:27,467 --> 00:04:28,821
Oh, and one more thing.

106
00:04:28,841 --> 00:04:30,493
This voice was made with AI.

107
00:04:30,513 --> 00:04:32,405
Now you know how to question it.

108
00:04:32,825 --> 00:04:33,919
See you in the comments.

